{"id": "506d1528-c23b-4698-880e-ab4fdad8e871", "timestamp": "2026-10-23T10:19:08.136409", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "35f0bc91-e2b9-465b-8d19-5c88abc00318", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Aprobación recibida. Añado una última comprobación de factibilidad para el análisis (los conteos se calcularían sobre dumps, sin torch) y entrego el diseño completo."}], "reasoning_content": "Good: networkx/numpy missing here, but graphlib (stdlib) available — so metric computation (topological order, longest path) can be pure-stdlib. That's a useful feasibility note: the analysis code for metrics is stdlib-only, so it's portable; only graph extraction needs torch.\n\nNow write the final design (stage=result). Comprehensive but organized. Include:\n\n0. Status: design-only, nothing executed.\n1. Verificado / recordado / supuesto separation table.\n2. Question + operative definitions (graph definition as design decision).\n3. Conditions ladder A/B/C/D/E with the pairwise comparison mapping.\n   - A: model-only graph (no optimizer) → metric-sensitivity control (null expected).\n   - B: elementary elementwise differentiable Adam, in-graph → baseline for structural counts.\n   - C: TorchOpt non-fused (primary library path) vs B → C1: library-rewrite effect (does TorchOpt's unfused implementation itself change graph size vs hand-written baseline?) and also C vs B sanity: same semantics.\n   - D: TorchOpt fused (primary, real) vs C → fusion effect at fixed library. Key comparison for the question.\n   - E: foreach=True (vectorized, non-fused) vs C → vectorization effect, isolating fusion from batching.\n   - D vs E: single-fused-node vs per-tensor-list-of-nodes: fusion vs vectorization contrast directly.\n   - Fallback D': custom_op stand-in, reported in separate row, never pooled with D.\n4. Graph \"in-graph\" mechanism: how the update enters the graph — two variants (G1 differentiable functional update: compute updated params with tensor ops under grad enabled, then compute a downstream loss' = f(updated params) and take grads → second-order graph containing update ops; G2 torch.autograd.grad through update). Need care: torch.optim.Adam's step is in-place under no_grad → cannot be in-graph; so B/C/D must use functional/differentiable update paths (TorchOpt's functional meta-optimizers are designed for this). Note: for the real TorchOpt fused CUDA kernel, differentiability through the update may not be supported → preflight test: does the fused path support being traced (needs a fake/meta grad registration). If it can't enter the graph at all, then that itself is the finding for the primary measurement: define Primary measurement = \"extended graph\" and Secondary measurement = \"as-shipped graph\" (standard no_grad step, graph = model backward only → identical for C and D → null by construction). This is a key honest design: two measurement regimes, R1 (shipped, no_grad) and R2 (differentiable update). Report both.\n5. Model task & data: fixed MLP, L ∈ {1,2,3,4} param tensors, synthetic fixed-seed data; repeat counts are census → 1 replicate, plus determinism re-run check (2 runs, assert byte-identical dumps).\n6. Metrics: complexity metrics list + interpretability proxies + a specific \"optimizer-attributed subgraph\" definition. Also semantic-op coverage metric.\n7. Controls: numerical equivalence preflight (params equal to tolerance after K steps), determinism, same extractor, param-count sweep, dtype/seed, negative control A, library control, vectorization control, and an ablation \"fused kernel opacity check\" (assert fused node has no children in the graph).\n8. Analysis plan: deterministic census, exact differences, ratios, slope vs L (linear fit of node counts as function of L; expected slope for fused ≈ 0 per param vs ~k per param for unfused), tables per comparison, no inferential stats for counts; decision rules/thresholds defined a priori; null taxonomy.\n9. Null taxonomy: nulo por construcción (R1 regime, or if fused op absent), nulo empírico (in R2 counts equal), and what's reported in each case.\n10. Human arm spec (secondary, pre-registered): n=8-10 raters, 4 figures, blinded/randomized order, rubric (identification of the node updating exp_avg, task time, NASA-TLX-free simple rating), Krippendorff's alpha, Wilcoxon signed-rank one-sided, minimum feasible n, declared as recommended extension, not required to answer question.\n11. Preflight checklist (executable elsewhere) with exact commands.\n12. Feasibility in this environment: nothing runnable; analysis code stdlib-only (verified graphlib works) so metric computation could run offline if given dumps.\n13. What the experiment cannot conclude (tautology limit).\n14. Deliverables/artifacts + preregistration.\n\nAlso worth noting: TorchViz cotejo (cross-check) procedure: compare node multiset from canonical traversal vs make_dot graph; record divergence as instrument-fidelity report; if divergence, report and prefer canonical.\n\nLet me write it. Keep it dense with headers. Spanish.\n\nNote the metrics list should include a defined formula: N_nodes, N_edges, U_labels (unique op names), Depth (longest directed path loss→leaf), N_opt (optimizer-attributed nodes), ratio N_opt/N, redundancy R = nodes not on any loss→leaf path, etc.\n\nCareful: In autograd graph, \"leaf\" = requires_grad tensors; the traversal from loss's grad_fn through next_functions yields GradEdges to tensors; nodes are NextFunctions; leaf nodes = AccumulateGrad. So \"loss→parameter paths\" means paths from root grad_fn to AccumulateGrad nodes. Good, and nodes not on any such path = dead-end branches (e.g., saved tensors shown as leaves by torchviz). TorchViz adds tensor nodes as leaves with names; that's part of the cotejo.\n\nAnalysis on Depth: fused collapses chains of update ops → depth reduction ~ number of elementwise ops in update.\n\nLet me write final answer. Also include a small table of comparisons.\n\nPreflight commands: pip install versions, python -c checks: torch.__version__, hasattr(torch, \"_fused_adam_\"), torch.ops.aten._fused_adam_.default, TORCHOPT.__version__, inspect.signature(torchopt.optim.Adam) / torchopt.adam, availability of fused on CPU: try instantiate & run 1 step with fused=True on CPU in try/except; TORCHOPT_BACKEND env var? unknown — mark as memory. torchviz.make_dot signature. graphviz `dot -V`. sha256 hashes of dumps.\n\nAlso note python 3.14 here may not be supported by torch wheels → memory-flagged; target env should use Python supported by the torch build (3.9–3.13 per memory).\n\nI'll finalize.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "python3 - <<'EOF'\n# Factibilidad del calculo de metricas SIN torch: solo stdlib sobre un dump JSON tipo.\nimport json, io\nfrom graphlib import TopologicalSorter\ng = json.loads('{\"nodes\":[\"L\",\"Mul\",\"Add\",\"AG_w\",\"AG_b\"],\"edges\":[[\"L\",\"Mul\"],[\"Mul\",\"Add\"],[\"Add\",\"AG_w\"],[\"Mul\",\"AG_b\"]]}')\nts = TopologicalSorter({n:set() for n in g[\"nodes\"]})\nfor a,b in g[\"edges\"]: ts.add_node(b,a)\norder=list(ts.static_order())\n# longitud de camino mas largo por DP (determinista, stdlib)\nbest={n:1 for n in g[\"nodes\"]}\nfor n in order:\n    for m in g[\"nodes\"]:\n        if [n,m] in g[\"edges\"]: best[m]=max(best[m],best[n]+1)\nprint(\"order\",order,\"depth\",max(best.values()),\"|V|\",len(g[\"nodes\"]),\"|E|\",len(g[\"edges\"]))\nEOF", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-9294283a4f4e2af5", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-9294283a4f4e2af5", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"python3 - <<'EOF'\\n# Factibilidad del calculo de metricas SIN torch: solo stdlib sobre un dump JSON tipo.\\nimport json, io\\nfrom graphlib import TopologicalSorter\\ng = json.loads('{\\\"nodes\\\":[\\\"L\\\",\\\"Mul\\\",\\\"Add\\\",\\\"AG_w\\\",\\\"AG_b\\\"],\\\"edges\\